{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"threads","title":"Threads: std::thread & std::jthread","group":"Concurrency","order":70,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>std::thread</code> runs a callable concurrently with the rest of the program. You must <code>join()</code> (wait for) or <code>detach()</code> it before it's destroyed; C++20's <code>std::jthread</code> joins automatically.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-play\"></i>Starting and joining</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nvoid partialSum(const std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; data, std::size_t from, std::size_t to, long long&amp; out) {\n    long long s = 0;\n    for (std::size_t i = from; i &lt; to; ++i) s += data[i];\n    out = s;                                          // each thread writes its own slot: no race\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; data(1'000'000, 1);\n    long long left = 0, right = 0;\n    std::size_t mid = data.size() / 2;\n\n    std::thread t1(partialSum, std::cref(data), 0, mid, std::ref(left));   // args are COPIED unless wrapped\n    std::thread t2([&amp;] { partialSum(data, mid, data.size(), right); });    // or capture in a lambda\n    t1.join();                                        // wait for both to finish\n    t2.join();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; left + right &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (std::thread::hardware_concurrency() &gt; 0) &lt;&lt; '\\n';        // number of cores (hint)\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1000000\n1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>If a joinable <code>std::thread</code> is destroyed (goes out of scope without <code>join()</code> or <code>detach()</code>), the program calls <code>std::terminate</code>. An exception thrown between creating and joining causes exactly this — which is why <code>std::jthread</code> exists.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-stop\"></i><code>std::jthread</code> (C++20)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int ticks = 0;\n    {\n        std::jthread worker([&amp;ticks](std::stop_token st) {   // receives a stop token\n            while (!st.stop_requested()) {\n                ++ticks;\n                std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(1));\n            }\n        });\n        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(20));\n    }   // ~jthread: request_stop() then join() — automatic\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"worker stopped after \" &lt;&lt; (ticks &gt; 0 ? \"some\" : \"no\") &lt;&lt; \" ticks\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>worker stopped after some ticks</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Thread basics</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>API</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::thread t(f, args...)</code></td><td>Start <code>f(args...)</code> on a new thread (arguments copied/moved into it)</td></tr><tr><td><code>t.join()</code></td><td>Block until <code>t</code> finishes</td></tr><tr><td><code>t.detach()</code></td><td>Let it run independently (you can no longer join it — rarely a good idea)</td></tr><tr><td><code>t.joinable()</code></td><td>Still represents a running/unjoined thread?</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::this_thread::sleep_for(d)</code> / <code>yield()</code> / <code>get_id()</code></td><td>Helpers for the current thread</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::ref(x)</code> / <code>std::cref(x)</code></td><td>Pass by reference into a thread</td></tr><tr><td><code>thread_local int x;</code></td><td>One separate variable per thread</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Output order is not deterministic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::jthread&gt; workers;\n    for (int id = 0; id &lt; 3; ++id)\n        workers.emplace_back([id] { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"worker \" &lt;&lt; id &lt;&lt; \" running\\n\"; });\n}   // lines may appear in any order — or even interleaved mid-line</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between a process and a thread?</summary><div><p>A process has its own address space; threads within a process share memory (globals, heap) but each has its own stack and registers. Threads are cheaper to create and communicate through shared memory — which is exactly why they need synchronisation.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if you forget to join a std::thread?</summary><div><p>When the <code>std::thread</code> object is destroyed while still joinable, its destructor calls <code>std::terminate()</code>, aborting the program. Use <code>std::jthread</code> (joins in its destructor) or an RAII guard.</p></div></details></section>"}